<p>First Presbyterian Church began as a home mission to Dakota Territory. {% person "isaac-oliver-sloan" %}Isaac Oliver Sloan{% endperson %} spent most of his life as a frontier pastor, traveling along the Northern Pacific Railway to preach and establish churches. In 1873, he helped establish a congregation in Bismarck and was elected its pastor. The <em>Mandan Pioneer</em> reported that "Rev. I. O. Sloan would preach at the school house" on September 11, 1880. The Mandan congregation initially met wherever space was available, including a saloon and the city's log schoolhouse. It eventually purchased property and constructed a church. Several Eastern congregations provided chairs and communion ware, and a congregation in Sloan's hometown of Philadelphia gave the church a large Bible.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-presbyterian-church/419-1919-first-presbyterian-church.jpg" alt="Line drawing of Mandan's first Presbyterian church building">
    <figcaption>Line drawing of the first church building</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The congregation was formally organized on August 21, 1881, when its first wooden church was completed near Dilworth Avenue, now Third Avenue NW, and Third Street NW. An 1883 edition of the <em>Mandan Pioneer</em> called it "the largest and most expensive house of worship in the city" and noted that "a large basement was furnished for a Sabbath School." {% person "c-a-heegard" %}C. A. Heegard{% endperson %} served as one of the early superintendents of the church school. Sloan was appointed Mandan's pastor in 1882.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-presbyterian-church/446-presbyterian-church-kms.jpg" alt="First Presbyterian Church in Mandan">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In June 1916, the original wooden church was moved across the street so services could continue while a new brick church was constructed on the original site. The cornerstone was laid in October 1916 before a large audience. The service opened with a hymn and a prayer by pastor {% person "f-w-thompson" %}F. W. Thompson{% endperson %}. {% person "c-h-brown" %}C. H. Brown{% endperson %} of Christ Episcopal Church read scripture, and {% person "f-w-gress" %}F. W. Gress{% endperson %} of the Methodist Church offered greetings. North Dakota Supreme Court Justice {% person "a-a-bruce" %}A. A. Bruce{% endperson %} laid the cornerstone.</p>

<p>A copper box placed beneath the stone contained a photograph of the old church; programs from the cornerstone ceremony and Thompson's installation; a list of church officers; and copies of the <em>Mandan Daily Pioneer</em>, <em>Mandan News</em>, and <em>Mandan Republican</em>. During construction, services were held in the Commercial Club rooms above the former Gambles store on Main Street.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/first-presbyterian-church/364-sloan-window-by-robert-porter.jpg" alt="Sloan Memorial stained-glass window">
    <figcaption>Sloan Memorial window</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The new church was formally dedicated with three services on Sunday, July 8, 1917. People from churches of all denominations joined the celebration. {% person "george-e-hunt" %}The Reverend George E. Hunt{% endperson %} of Wisconsin was the special speaker, and Pastor Thompson officiated. The congregation admired the building's exterior, eight stained-glass windows, 350 oak seats, and richly furnished interior. The day's highlight came at the conclusion of the late-afternoon service, when the setting sun streamed through the Sloan Memorial window above the main entrance.</p>

<p>Other ministers included {% person "hugh-h-owen" %}the Reverend Hugh H. Owen{% endperson %} and {% person "i-scherpenisse" %}the Reverend I. Scherpenisse{% endperson %}. The {% inflation 35000, 1917 %} building was fully paid for when it opened. Fundraising continued, producing another $5,000 for a pipe organ installed in July 1919.</p>

<p>In 1957, a south wing added two floors of classrooms, a small chapel, church offices, and a pastor's study. {% person "john-kuyper" %}The Reverend John Kuyper{% endperson %} and {% person "lyon-cary" %}Mrs. Lyon Cary{% endperson %}, a parish member since 1889, hosted the groundbreaking ceremony. {% person "george-toman" %}George Toman{% endperson %} was school superintendent. {% person "gilbert-w-stewart" %}Gilbert W. Stewart{% endperson %} had served as pastor for the preceding 25 years. {% person "ed-toman" %}Ed Toman{% endperson %} chaired the Building Committee, and {% person "w-a-wise" %}W. A. Wise{% endperson %} chaired the Education Committee.</p>

